    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=32$, so $y=\\dfrac{32}{x^2}$.\n\nMissing entries, from left to right: $x=\\pm4$, $y=0.5$, $x=\\pm10$, $y=0.125$.\n\nThe answer book selects $x=4$ and $x=10$; these are the positive solutions.",
      "worked": "Write $y=\\dfrac{k}{x^2}$. Using $x=2$ and $y=8$,\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=x^2y\\\\k&=2^2\\times8\\\\k&=32.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $y=\\dfrac{32}{x^2}$.\n\nWhen $y=2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=\\frac{32}{2}\\\\x^2&=16\\\\x&=\\pm4.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=8$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{32}{8^2}\\\\y&=\\frac12=0.5.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $y=0.32$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=\\frac{32}{0.32}\\\\x^2&=100\\\\x&=\\pm10.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=16$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{32}{16^2}\\\\y&=\\frac18=0.125.\\end{align*}$$\nFor positive $x$, the completed rows are $x: 2,4,8,10,16$ and $y: 8,2,0.5,0.32,0.125$. Without a positive-domain restriction, either sign is valid in each missing $x$ cell.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
